{
  "image_name": "4f485ba09d86d28de27039025e5b01d4.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure is a schematic of intestinal wall inflammation, showing how epithelial damage, bacteria, immune cells, fibroblasts, smooth muscle, and mesenteric fat interact across tissue layers. At the top, injury to the epithelium appears to weaken the barrier, allowing bacteria and microbial products to enter the tissue. Signals from the damaged epithelium, including cytokines such as IL-33, IL-34, and IL-11, are directed toward stromal cells beneath the surface.\n\nThe center of the figure emphasizes fibroblast heterogeneity. Different fibroblast subsets are marked by distinct gene signatures and receptor pathways, suggesting that multiple fibroblast states respond to inflammatory and microbial cues. These cells seem to receive input both from immune cells and from bacteria-related sensing pathways, then influence tissue properties through cadherin-11, extracellular matrix composition, and matrix stiffness.\n\nLower in the tissue, the muscular layer and mesenteric fat are also involved. Immune cells and inflammatory mediators extend into these deeper regions, while fat cells release free fatty acids and pro-inflammatory, pro-fibrotic cytokines. Overall, the image conveys a feed-forward process in which barrier injury, microbial invasion, immune activation, fibroblast remodeling, and “creeping fat” together drive chronic intestinal inflammation and fibrosis.",
